# ENG-CAT-001 — Core Platform Engine Catalog Governance

## Preserve the Catalog

1. **Never remove ENG-001 – ENG-019** from architecture documentation.
   - Canonical catalog: `01-enterprise-architecture/02-Platform-Module-Catalog.md` §3
   - Architecture detail: `01-enterprise-architecture/01-Enterprise-Solution-Architecture.md` §5 (v1.0 Platform Engine Baseline)
2. Do not collapse, rename, or delete engine IDs without following the merge workflow below.

## v1.0 Platform Engine Baseline

- **Active baseline:** ENG-001 – ENG-016 (see catalog §3)
- **Phase 2 (introduce only when needed):** ENG-017 – ENG-019
- **Extension IDs:** ENG-015a (compliance layer on ENG-015); ENG-003a–n (Configuration Engine sub-engines); **next: ENG-003o**

## AV-1.5 Engine Catalog Lock (LOCKED)

Effective until AV-2.0 is deliberately initiated:

| Rule | Requirement |
|------|-------------|
| No renumbering | Do not rename or reassign existing engine IDs under AV-1.x |
| No regrouping | Do not create engine families or restructure ENG-003 under AV-1.x |
| New capabilities | Assign **ENG-003o**, **ENG-003p**, … — record in catalog §3 first |
| AV-2.0 only | Regrouping requires AV-2.0 approval + migration map + Merge Registry |

See `01-enterprise-architecture/01b-Architecture-Versions.md` — AV-1.5 Engine Catalog Lock and AV-2.0 review triggers.

## Duplicate Detection & Merge Workflow

When two or more engine IDs describe the **same capability** (e.g. ENG-017 and ENG-003b both = Localization & Regulatory):

### Step 1 — Compare (mandatory)

Side-by-side diff of every duplicate:

- Purpose statement
- Core responsibilities / scope
- Configuration dimensions
- Integration points
- Implementation location
- Build Pack references

### Step 2 — Merge missing content (mandatory)

- Identify responsibilities present in the **newer or duplicate** ID but absent from the **canonical** ID.
- Port those additions into the canonical engine specification.
- Output **one merged engine definition** — never leave two parallel specs.

### Step 3 — Present for approval (mandatory — do not skip)

Before replacing or removing any engine entry, present to the user:

```
Engine Merge Proposal — AWAITING APPROVAL

Canonical:  ENG-003b — Localization & Regulatory Engine
Retiring:   ENG-017 — Localization & Regulatory Engine

Additions ported into canonical:
- [list each item]

Proposed merged output:
[full single engine definition]

Files to update:
- [list files]

Approve merge? (yes/no)
```

**Do not apply documentation or code changes until the user explicitly approves.**

### Step 4 — Record the merge

After approval:

1. Update the canonical engine spec with merged content.
2. Remove the duplicate from active catalog tables (§3 in Platform Module Catalog).
3. Add a row to **§3.1 Engine Merge Registry** with retired ID, canonical ID, rationale, and absorbed content.
4. Leave a one-line pointer in the canonical spec: `> Merge note: ENG-XXX merged into ENG-YYY`.
5. Add or update an entry in **Architecture Versions** (`01-enterprise-architecture/01b-Architecture-Versions.md`) with from → to and reasoning.

## Canonical vs Extension IDs

| Pattern | Treatment |
|---------|-----------|
| ENG-003a–n | Sub-engines under ENG-003; keep as separate rows |
| ENG-015a | Extension of ENG-015 (compliance layer); keep both unless user approves merge |
| ENG-017 (Localization duplicate) → ENG-003b | Merged; retired ID lives only in §3.1 Merge Registry |
| ENG-017 (Phase 2) | Identity Resolution Engine — distinct future capability; not the retired ID |
| ENG-010 → ENG-003e | Merged; integration scope under Enterprise Integration Engine |

## Related Engines (not duplicates)

Do not merge without explicit approval when engines are **complementary**:

- ENG-003b (regulatory config) + ENG-003i (consent events)
- ENG-015 (document generation) + ENG-015a (compliance scoring)
- ENG-003c (org structure) + ENG-003b (localization)
- ENG-003l (checklist completion) + ENG-015a (document compliance matching) — complementary; checklist items consume compliance state
- ENG-003l (checklist completion) + ENG-005 (workflow) — complementary; checklists gate progression; workflow orchestrates approvals
- ENG-003m (portfolio roadmap) + ENG-003f (product intelligence) — complementary; ENG-003m owns planning entities; ENG-003f analyses outcomes

## AV-2.0 — Future engine family regrouping (planning only)

ENG-003 was originally Platform Foundation / Metadata. It now has **14 sub-engines** (003a–n). v1.0 keeps flat sub-engine IDs — this is valid and governed above.

When the platform outgrows a single ENG-003 family, **AV-2.0** may introduce logical groupings, for example:

- **ENG-003 Platform Metadata Services** — Configuration (003a), Localization (003b), Organization (003c), …
- **Platform Intelligence Services** (family ID TBD) — Checklist (003l → 017a), Portfolio (003m → 017b), Product Intelligence (003f → 017c), Event Ingestion (003d → 017d), …

**Do not regroup or renumber under AV-1.x** — AV-1.5 Engine Catalog Lock is in force. New sub-engines: **ENG-003o**, **ENG-003p**, …

AV-2.0 review triggers (see Architecture Versions): ENG-003 reaches 20 sub-engines → review required; >3 capability domains → consider regrouping; multi-team ownership boundaries → create families; AI misclassification → review taxonomy.

Any AV-2.0 change requires user approval, full ID migration map, Merge Registry updates, and resolution of ENG-017 (currently Phase 2 Identity Resolution Engine).

See `01-enterprise-architecture/01b-Architecture-Versions.md` — Future Architecture Considerations (AV-2.0).

